{
  "role": "novelty",
  "novelty": 3,
  "explanation": "The proposal introduces a creative conceptual combination: translating a recent single-model latent probing and steering technique designed for novelty judgment calibration (Think-Probe-Respond, Schopf et al., 2026) into an active generation-time steering mechanism within a multi-agent ideation loop. While SciMON (Wang et al., 2024) explores literature-grounded novelty optimization through text-level iterative revision, and Schopf et al. (2026.findings-emnlp.574) use hidden-state probing to fix post-hoc LLM judgment bias, applying latent probing directly to steer the generation agent's decoding phase represents a distinct architectural shift. However, the novelty is somewhat tempered because it directly applies existing probe-based internal representation correction methods to a new agent role without introducing fundamentally new probing mechanics or addressing core limitations of linear probing over multi-step generational decoding.",
  "problems": [
    "The core methodological transfer relies heavily on taking a post-hoc judgment calibration technique (Think-Probe-Respond) and repurposing it for active text generation, without explaining how static novelty-tier classifiers generalize to intermediate, unfinished conceptual representations during generation decoding.",
    "Training linear probes requires explicit novelty-tier gold labels for hidden states, but intermediate generation steps lack ground-truth novelty labels, raising questions about how the training targets for the probes are constructed during generation.",
    "The proposal assumes that a 4-bit quantized Llama-3-8B-Instruct model on a 12GB consumer GPU can comfortably perform real-time hidden state extraction, linear probe inference, and activation steering/token modulation loops during multi-agent generation without severe latency or memory bottlenecks."
  ],
  "improvements": [
    "Clarify the training data and supervision signal used to train the linear probes for intermediate generation steps, distinguishing them from the final-answer judgment probes described in Schopf et al. (2026.findings-emnlp.574).",
    "Provide a concrete mathematical or algorithmic formulation of how the continuous probed novelty vector modifies the generator's decoding logits or activations (e.g., specifying whether steering vectors are applied via activation addition or token-level bias).",
    "Incorporate baseline comparisons against standard prompt-based negative constraints or classifier-guided decoding (e.g., guided generation using external reward models) to isolate the specific benefit of latent-space hidden-state probing over standard control methods."
  ],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "paper_id": "2024.acl-long.18",
      "claim": "SciMON performs literature-grounded scientific ideation and optimizes for novelty through iterative text-level comparison.",
      "supporting_excerpt": "We present SCIMON, a modeling framework that uses retrieval of “inspirations” from past scientific papers, and explicitly optimizes for novelty by iteratively comparing to prior papers and updating idea suggestions until sufficient novelty is achieved.",
      "relation": "context"
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "2026.findings-emnlp.574",
      "claim": "Think-Probe-Respond uses latent hidden state probing to mitigate medium novelty bias in single-model judgments.",
      "supporting_excerpt": "To mitigate this, we propose ThinkProbe-Respond (TPR), a lightweight approach that probes latent novelty judgments from hidden states during the reasoning phase and uses the probed judgments to condition the final response.",
      "relation": "overlaps"
    }
  ],
  "uncertainty": "It remains uncertain whether linear probes extracted from a critic's reasoning phase can be effectively transferred to steer a separate generator agent's decoding phase without causing semantic incoherence or degradation in technical depth."
}
